============================================================
ABLATION CONCLUSION
============================================================

1. ACCURACY: Attribute smoothing contributed ZERO measurable improvement.
   Both runs peaked at exactly 91.75% val top-1.
   - Baseline reached 91.75% at epoch 24
   - Phase 2e reached 91.75% at epoch 19 (5 epochs earlier)
   - Phase 2e arrived at peak slightly faster, but the ceiling is identical.

2. CALIBRATION: Attribute smoothing HURT model calibration significantly.
   - Baseline val loss: 1.163 (flat, healthy range 1.163-1.172)
   - Phase 2e val loss: 1.269 and RISING (started at 1.199, increased every epoch after 17)
   - Delta: Baseline has 8-10% LOWER val loss across all epochs after warmup
   - Phase 2e showed continuous val loss degradation (1.199 -> 1.275) despite flat accuracy
   - This means attribute smoothing made the model LESS well-calibrated:
     the predicted probabilities became less reliable even though argmax accuracy was the same.

3. OVERFITTING BEHAVIOR:
   - Phase 2e: val loss increased monotonically from epoch 17 to 30 (13 consecutive increases).
     This is textbook overfitting of the loss function, masked by accuracy plateau.
   - Baseline: val loss is FLAT (1.163-1.172 range, no trend). Much healthier training dynamics.
   - The attribute smoothing loss function was harder for the model to fit cleanly,
     causing the loss to drift upward even as the model maintained accuracy.

4. TRAINING LOSS COMPARISON:
   - Baseline train loss: 1.53-1.77 range (MixUp/CutMix inflated, standard CE)
   - Phase 2e train loss: 0.77-1.17 range (attribute smoothing uses different loss scale)
   - The loss scales are not directly comparable due to different loss functions.
   - However, Phase 2e train loss continued to decrease (1.17 -> 0.77) while val loss
     increased, indicating the model was memorizing training patterns. Classic overfit signature.
   - Baseline train loss was stable (1.53-1.57), indicating no memorization.

5. WHAT THE 384px RESOLUTION INCREASE ACTUALLY DID:
   - Phase 1 (224px): 90.87% best
   - Phase 2 baseline (384px, no attr smooth): 91.75% best
   - Phase 2e (384px, with attr smooth): 91.75% best
   - Resolution increase: +0.88 pp (the ENTIRE gain)
   - Attribute smoothing: +0.00 pp (ZERO contribution)

============================================================
LOSS ANALYSIS (BASELINE RUN DETAILED)
============================================================

Val loss trajectory:
- Epoch 16 (warmup): 1.2545 -- starting point from resumed checkpoint
- Epochs 17-19: 1.1734 -> 1.1722 -> 1.1692 -- rapid decrease, model adapting to 384px
- Epochs 19-28: oscillating 1.1629-1.1714 -- stable plateau, no degradation
- Best val loss: 1.1629 at epoch 28 -- model STILL slowly improving loss late in training
- No consecutive increases of 3+ epochs. Val loss is healthy.

Train-val gap:
- Average train loss: ~1.55 (inflated by MixUp/CutMix)
- Average val loss: ~1.17
- Gap: ~0.38 -- consistent throughout training, no widening. Healthy.
- The gap is expected: MixUp/CutMix create harder training targets (soft labels),
  so train loss is always higher than val loss. This is NOT overfitting.

LR schedule correlation:
- Epoch 16: LR=5e-08 (warmup). Minimal learning, baseline metrics.
- Epoch 17: LR=5e-06 (peak). Biggest single-epoch gain (+1.13 pp accuracy, -0.081 val loss).
- Epochs 18-28: cosine decay 4.95e-06 -> 1.44e-06. Gradual refinement.
- Model showed fastest improvement at peak LR, then diminishing returns as LR decayed.
- This is textbook cosine schedule behavior -- no anomalies.

Calibration check:
- Val loss and val accuracy are generally correlated (lower loss = higher accuracy).
- Slight decorrelation in later epochs: loss varies 1.163-1.172 while accuracy varies 91.25-91.75%.
- Normal at plateau -- small logit changes flip a few predictions without changing loss much.
- No calibration divergence. The baseline is well-calibrated.

Early stopping analysis:
- Best accuracy: epoch 24 (91.75%). With patience=7, early stopping would trigger at epoch 31.
- Best loss: epoch 28 (1.1629). Model was still improving loss metric at end of training.
- The 30-epoch budget was appropriate. No epochs were wasted.

============================================================
RECOMMENDATIONS FOR NEXT STEPS
============================================================

1. ABANDON ATTRIBUTE SMOOTHING. The ablation proves it adds complexity with zero accuracy
   benefit and worse calibration. Remove from the pipeline.

2. DO NOT REPEAT 384px RUNS. The ceiling at 384px appears to be ~91.75% with this model
   and training setup. Both approaches (with and without attr smoothing) hit the same wall.

3. RESOLUTION IS THE PROVEN LEVER. 224px -> 384px gave +0.88 pp. Next step:
   - 448px was tested in output_20260314_083230 with attr smoothing, also 91.75%
   - The 448px run also hit 91.75%, suggesting resolution gains are saturating.

4. TO BREAK 92%, CONSIDER:
   a. Test-Time Augmentation (TTA): average predictions over 5-10 augmented views. Free accuracy.
   b. Stochastic Weight Averaging (SWA): average weights from epochs 20-28 plateau region.
   c. Model ensemble: combine ConvNeXt V2 with EVA-02 or different architecture.
   d. Larger model: ConvNeXt V2 Large (if VRAM permits) or EVA-02 Large.
   e. Different fine-tuning strategy: layer-wise LR decay, longer warmup.
   f. Data augmentation changes: try RandAugment, AugMix, or stronger color jitter.

5. CALIBRATION MATTERS FOR DEPLOYMENT. The baseline has significantly better calibration
   (val loss 1.163 vs 1.269). If probability estimates matter (not just argmax), the baseline
   model is strictly superior to the attribute-smoothed version.

============================================================
DOES THIS CHANGE PROJECT STRATEGY?
============================================================

YES. Key implications:

1. Attribute smoothing was a dead end. The custom loss function added code complexity,
   required attribute data loading, and produced WORSE calibration for ZERO accuracy gain.
   Revert to standard CrossEntropyLoss + label_smoothing=0.1.

2. The 91.75% ceiling appears robust. Three independent runs (baseline 384px, attr smooth 384px,
   attr smooth 448px) all converged to exactly 91.75%. This is likely near the single-model
   ceiling for ConvNeXt V2 Base on CUB-200-2011 with current augmentation strategy.

3. To push beyond 91.75%, the project needs a DIFFERENT approach:
   - TTA and SWA are low-hanging fruit (no retraining needed)
   - Ensembling multiple architectures is the most reliable path to 92-93%+
   - Larger models or different pretraining (DINOv2, CLIP) could shift the ceiling

4. The baseline model from this run is the BEST model to keep:
   same accuracy, better calibration, simpler pipeline.

